<article class="page-content">
  <h1>About BSF Indoors</h1>

  <section>
    <h2>What this site is</h2>
    <p>
      BSF Indoors documents a black soldier fly larvae (BSFL) colony kept
      <strong>year-round indoors</strong> in Cache Valley, Utah — USDA Zone 5b,
      ~4,500 ft elevation, where outdoor BSF is a no-go from October through
      April. Every guide, gear pick, and protocol on this site comes from a
      working rig: a butterfly-mesh love cage, a quartz halogen worklight as
      the indoor mating lamp, a self-harvest larvae bin, and a separate
      pupation chamber.
    </p>
    <p>
      The site exists because most BSF resources are written for tropical or
      sub-tropical climates and quietly assume sunlight, ambient warmth, and
      humidity that cold-climate operators don't get for half the year.
      What works in Florida doesn't always work in a basement at 4,500 ft.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Why "indoor" gets its own site</h2>
    <p>
      Three things change once you commit to an indoor colony:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Mating without sunlight.</strong> Adults won't copulate without
        the right wavelengths and intensity. A cheap halogen worklight with the
        UV filter glass removed gets you ~61% of natural sunlight efficacy
        (Tomberlin &amp; Sheppard 2010) — but only with the right distance,
        photoperiod, and cage geometry.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Climate the flies, not the room.</strong> The cage interior
        needs to hold 25–32&nbsp;°C and 50–60% RH. In a Cache Valley basement
        running 22&nbsp;°C and 28% RH, that's a microclimate problem, not a
        room-thermostat problem. Different gear, different approach.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Smell, escapees, and shared rooms.</strong> Indoor BSF lives
        next to your network rack, your laundry, or your spare bedroom. The
        operational decisions look completely different from "set up a barrel
        in the backyard."
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How recommendations get made here</h2>
    <p>
      The product picks on this site come from one of three places:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>In use on the rig.</strong> If we recommend a worklight, hygrometer, or self-harvest bin, we own it and it's running right now. Photos in the guides are of the actual unit.</li>
      <li><strong>Tested and rejected.</strong> When something didn't work, we say so and document why. Failure modes are at least as useful as wins.</li>
      <li><strong>Cited from peer-reviewed entomology and university extensions.</strong> Numbers in the guides (temperature, humidity, lux, ramp angle, pupation time) trace back to specific papers in our knowledge base — Tomberlin, Sheppard, Bertinetti, Diener, Oonincx, Hoc, and the FAO/Eawag field manuals.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Anything that's <em>not</em> from one of those three sources is flagged as
      such, and we generally don't write about it until we've used it.
    </p>
  </section>

